==English==

===Etymology===
From {{uder|en|fro|desplaisir}}. See {{m|en|displease}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{a|UK}} {{IPA|en|/dɪsˈplɛʒə/}}
* {{a|US}} {{enPR|dĭs-plĕzhʹər}}, {{IPA|en|/dɪsˈplɛʒɚ/}}
* {{rhymes|en|ɛʒə(ɹ)|s=3}}
* {{audio|en|LL-Q1860 (eng)-Vealhurl-displeasure.wav |Audio (Southern England)}}

===Noun===
{{en-noun|-|s}}

# A [[feeling]] of being [[displease]]d with something or someone; [[dissatisfaction]]; [[disapproval]].
#* {{quote-journal|en|date=October 20, 2011|author=Michael da Silva|title=Stoke 3 - 0 Macc Tel-Aviv| work=BBC Sport| url=http://news.bbc.co.uk/sport2/hi/football/europe/15285214.stm| passage=Tangling with Ziv, Cameron caught him with a flailing elbow, causing the Israeli defender to go down a little easily. However, the referee was in no doubt, much to the '''displeasure''' of the home fans.}}
# That which displeases; cause of irritation or annoyance; offence; injury.
#* {{RQ:Shakespeare Comedy of Errors|IV|iv|passage=Hast thou delight to see a wretched man / Do outrage and '''displeasure''' to himself?}}
# A state of [[disgrace]] or [[disfavour]].
#* {{RQ:Shakespeare King Lear|III|iii|passage=[King Lear] charged me, on pain of their perpetual '''displeasure''', neither to speak of him [Edgar], entreat for him, nor any way sustain him.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1622|author=Henry Peacham|title=The compleat gentleman fashioning him absolute in the most necessary & commendable qualities concerning minde or bodie that may be required in a noble gentleman.|page=101|passage=[H]ee went into Poland, being in '''displeasure''' with the pope for ouermuch familiaritie with a kinswoman of his.|url=https://quod.lib.umich.edu/e/eebo/A09195.0001.001/114:16?vid=14713}}

